THE ONE RULE
TradeBook is a two-sided marketplace, not a consumer app.
Liquidity is per category, per metro, per time window — not per state.
Eight Birmingham homes posting removals and six removal vendors accepting
beats 80 accounts across three states and seven job types with zero awards.
Narrow the unit until you can win it. Density first. Ads last.

THE SECOND RULE
Speed is the product for urgent work. A director with a body in a nursing
home at 2 a.m. will not wait for three bids. If the book cannot beat his
speed-dial, the book loses — no matter how good the pricing screen is.


1. WHAT WE ARE ACTUALLY LAUNCHING
Funeral homes and cemeteries post trade work. Vendors respond. The home
decides. Cheapest is a sort, never an automatic award.

TWO LANES — THIS IS THE CORE PRODUCT CHANGE

  URGENT LANE (first call / removal / cooler down / machine down before a viewing)
  - Vendor sets a standing rate + coverage radius once, at signup.
  - Home posts. Qualified vendors get a push/SMS. First to ACCEPT takes it.
  - Home confirms with one tap. Target: accepted in under 10 minutes.
  - No auction. No waiting. Home can still decline and re-open.

  BID LANE (trade embalming, scheduled transport, PM, vault, equipment)
  - Post → vendors bid price + ETA + method (on-site / mail-in) as ONE offer.
  - Home clicks Award. App never auto-picks cheapest.
  - This is the existing flow and it is good. Keep it.

THE RECORD (why they come back)
Every job — sourced on TradeBook or not — produces a record: who, when,
price agreed, ETA met or missed, custody handoff timestamped, photo/POD,
signature, exportable invoice and 1099-ready totals.
Homes may log a direct-called job on TradeBook for free. This is deliberate.
The book must be useful on job #2 even when they already know the vendor.
If TradeBook is only a matching screen, we die at 40% repeat.

